activation	Print prompt	2026-10-28T05:10:15.5341792Z {"title": "After upgrading DuckDB, a delete-heavy transactional stage became significantly slower on the same workload shape", "body": "### What happens?\n\nAfter upgrading DuckDB from 1.4 to 1.5.2, a delete-heavy transactional stage became significantly slower on the same workload shape (I didn't change a single line!)\n\nThe pattern is:\n\nMark a large subset of rows for deletion.\nDelete related rows from a connection table using IN (SELECT ...).\nDelete primary rows.\nDelete dangling rows from a net table.\nCommit one large transaction.\nWe run specific recipes/pipelines in our system.\nObserved runtime regression in our workload (same hardware/config class, same 65GB DB), version baseline(1.4.0) vs new (1.5.2):\n\nminimal delete set: 277.26svs 550.44s\nmaximum delete set: 549.31s vs 1570.54s # worst impact\n\n### To Reproduce\n\nconn.execute(\"BEGIN\")\n\n    conn.execute(\n        f\"\"\"\n        DELETE FROM pex_connection\n        WHERE pex_element_id IN (SELECT id FROM pex_element WHERE delete)\n        \"\"\",\n    )\n\n    conn.execute(\n        f\"\"\"\n        DELETE FROM pex_element\n        WHERE delete\n        \"\"\",\n    )\n\n    conn.execute(\n        f\"\"\"\n        DELETE FROM pex_net\n        WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT pex_net_id FROM pex_connection)\n        \"\"\",\n    )\n\n    conn.execute(\"COMMIT\") # 95% of the time is consumed here\n\n\n### OS:\n\nLinux 5.15.0-177-generic x86_64 (Ubuntu)\n\n### DuckDB Version:\n\n1.4.0 (baseline) vs 1.5.2 (regression observed)\n\n### DuckDB Client:\n\nPython\n\n### Hardware:\n\nIntel Xeon 6724P, 32 CPUs (16 cores / 2 threads), 503 GiB RAM\n\n### Full Name:\n\nAmmar Shahin\n\n### Affiliation:\n\nLENS\n\n### Did you include all relevant configuration (e.g., CPU architecture, Linux distribution) to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [x] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all code required to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [x] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all relevant data sets for reproducing the issue?\n\nNo - I cannot share the data sets because they are confidential"}
